* /**
*  * @file GIT.CLONE
*  * @version 1.0
*  */
SUBROUTINE GIT.CLONE
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
* The include is REQUIRED now and was not before, which is exactly how this
* handler broke.  It used to be `$IFDEF MVX / $ELSE / $ENDIF`, and with MVX
* undefined the $ELSE arm ran whether or not any define had been seen — so the
* file worked without ever including PLATFORM.H.  Splitting that into three
* POSITIVE $IFDEFs removed the fallback: with no defines in scope, none matched,
* and the whole body disappeared at compile time.  The symptom was a handler
* that was entered, parsed its arguments, and then did nothing at all.
CALL GIT.SENT(S)
URL = FIELD(S, " ", 3)
DIR = FIELD(S, " ", 4)
REF = FIELD(S, " ", 5)
IF URL = "" OR DIR = "" THEN
   PRINT "usage: GIT CLONE url dir {ref}"
   RETURN
END
* THE PLATFORM IS DECIDED AT COMPILE TIME BUT ACTED ON AT RUNTIME.
*
* This handler used to put each platform's whole body inside its own $IFDEF, and
* on UniVerse the conditional body silently did not run — the subroutine was
* entered, the sentence parsed, and then nothing happened at all (mv_git#52).
* Every ingredient works in isolation: $IFDEF UV resolves, a cataloged
* subroutine called via CALL @H runs its conditional body, and EXECUTE …
* CAPTURING works inside one.  Only the assembly failed, at this nesting depth.
*
* So the conditionals now do one thing each — set a name — and the work happens
* in ordinary runtime code.  That is a smaller thing to ask of the preprocessor,
* and it turns the failure mode inside out: if the defines do not reach here the
* handler SAYS so instead of returning in silence, which is what made this cost
* an afternoon.
PLAT = ""
$IFDEF MVX
PLAT = "MVX"
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
PLAT = "UDT"
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UV
PLAT = "UV"
$ENDIF
IF PLAT = "" THEN
   PRINT "GIT CLONE: this build defines no platform - check BP.INC PLATFORM.H"
   RETURN
END
BEGIN CASE
   CASE PLAT = "MVX"
$IFDEF MVX
*     The engine's own clone: MVX materialises straight from the git objects.
      OUT = ""
      CALL GITCLONE(URL, DIR, REF, OUT)
      PRINT OUT
$ENDIF
   CASE PLAT = "UV"
*     CLONE IS THE ONE OPERATION A SESSION CANNOT DO FOR ITSELF.  Every other
*     verb works on the account it is running in; clone creates an account that
*     does not exist yet — a UniVerse account is born by running `uv` in an empty
*     directory and answering for a flavour — and this session is inside a
*     different account entirely.
*
*     The engine's plain clone is worse than useless here: a native account's
*     records live at VOC/<id>, so checking them out writes a DIRECTORY called
*     VOC, and UniVerse can then never create its own VOC hash file because the
*     name is taken.  The checkout blocks the account it was meant to become.
*
*     uv-git already does this correctly, so the verb hands over rather than
*     keeping a second, broken implementation.  The developer still types
*     GIT CLONE at the TCL prompt and never sees a shell.
      CMD = "uv-git clone " : URL : " " : DIR
      IF REF # "" THEN CMD := " " : REF
*     2>&1 because uv-git reports progress and failures on stderr and only the
*     final line on stdout — CAPTURING takes stdout alone, so without this a
*     clone that FAILED came back as silence.
      CMD := " 2>&1"
      EXECUTE "SH -c '" : CMD : "'" CAPTURING COUT
      FOR CI = 1 TO DCOUNT(COUT, @AM)
         IF TRIM(COUT<CI>) # "" THEN PRINT COUT<CI>
      NEXT CI
   CASE 1
*     UniData: the engine's clone over CallC.
      CALL GIT.REPO(REPO)
      CALL GIT.OBJ("CLONE", 4, REPO, URL, DIR, REF, "", OBJOUT)
      CALL GIT.ECHO(OBJOUT)
END CASE
RETURN